with d as (select value r from jsonb_array_elements($q$[{"notes":["Wild fennel (fronda di finocchietto) is the leafy fronds, not the seeds. In Australia, you may find it frozen in the Asian or Italian section; if not, a small amount of fresh dill mixed with a pinch of ground fennel seed is an acceptable substitute, though the taste won't be quite the same.","The pasta can be made up to 2 hours ahead and kept covered on the bench. You can also freeze it on a tray, then bag it; cook from frozen, adding 1–2 minutes to the cooking time.","If fresh sardines are hard to find, use tinned sardines in oil (drain well and add them to the sauce at the end, just to warm through, so they don't fall apart).","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"steps":[{"text":"Make the pasta: pile the flour on a clean bench, make a well in the centre and crack the eggs into it. With a fork, beat the eggs lightly, then gradually incorporate the flour, mixing with your fingers until a shaggy dough forms. Knead for 10 minutes until smooth and elastic. Wrap in plastic wrap and rest for 30 minutes."},{"text":"While the dough rests, steep the saffron in the warm water until the colour is deep gold, about 5 minutes."},{"text":"In a small frying pan over medium heat, lightly toast the pine nuts, shaking often, until fragrant, about 3 minutes. Set aside."},{"text":"Heat the olive oil in a large frying pan over medium heat. Add the sliced onion and cook gently, stirring, until soft and golden, about 8 minutes. Add the chopped garlic and cook for another minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the fresh sardines (or break them into large pieces if very large) to the pan and cook gently, stirring carefully, until the flesh is opaque and beginning to flake, about 5 minutes. Pour in the saffron water and add the chopped wild fennel. Stir gently and simmer for 5 minutes. Season with salt and black pepper. Turn off the heat."},{"text":"Bring a large pot of salted water to a boil until it is at a rolling, bubbling boil."},{"text":"Roll out the pasta dough thinly (about 2 mm) on a floured bench. If you have a pasta machine, use it. Cut into long, irregular ribbons or shapes, traditional pasta ca' le sardine is not uniform; think rustic and handmade."},{"text":"Cook the pasta in the boiling water, stirring gently, until al dente, about 4–5 minutes for fresh pasta. (Test a piece; it should have a slight bite.)"},{"text":"Reserve 200 ml of the pasta cooking water, then drain the pasta."},{"text":"Gently reheat the sardine sauce over medium-low heat. Add the cooked pasta to the sauce and toss carefully, adding pasta water a little at a time to loosen the sauce and help it coat the pasta. The pasta should look silky, not dry or soupy."},{"text":"Divide among bowls, scatter the toasted pine nuts over the top, and serve at once."}],"id":"7b283118-9d36-40b4-b3e2-8fe3fcf54128"},{"notes":["The key is steam. Wrap that lid in a towel, no shortcuts. If your kitchen is very cold, you may need another 5 minutes.","Listen for the gentle crackling around 15 minutes in. That's how you know it's working.","If the tahdig is too dark or burns, it happens. Serve it anyway, bitter tahdig with yoghurt is still tahdig."],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the basmati rice under cold water until the water runs mostly clear. This removes excess starch. Soak in cold water for 30 minutes, then drain."},{"text":"Bring a large pot of salted water (like the sea) to a boil. Add the drained rice and cook uncovered for about 8 minutes, until the grains are just starting to soften on the outside but still firm in the centre. The rice should be only half-cooked."},{"text":"Drain the rice well through a fine sieve, shaking gently to remove excess water."},{"text":"Melt the butter in the same pot over medium heat. Add the drained rice, stirring gently to coat with butter. Spread it in an even layer. Make a well in the centre with the back of a spoon."},{"text":"Wrap the lid tightly in a clean kitchen towel so the steam stays trapped, then place it firmly on the pot. Reduce the heat to medium-low and cook for 30 minutes. You want to hear a gentle crackling sound after about 15 minutes, that's the tahdig forming."},{"text":"Remove from heat. Let it rest for 5 minutes with the lid still on. Carefully run a thin knife around the edges of the pot, then place a large serving platter upside-down over the pot and flip everything out in one confident motion. The rice will mound on the platter with the golden tahdig crust on top."},{"text":"Serve the tahdig hot with cold Greek yoghurt on the side. Break off pieces of the crispy rice and eat it together with a spoonful of yoghurt. This is the prize."}],"id":"0bdf6c65-9c69-469a-9959-8c705b700435"},{"notes":["Cook the shrimp in batches to avoid crowding the pan, this ensures even cooking and a proper sear.","Stone-ground grits take longer than instant grits and have better flavour. Don't skip the low-and-slow cooking.","Food safety: Cook bacon and cured pork until the fat is rendered and the meat is hot right through. Cook prawns and other crustaceans until the shells turn pink and the flesh is opaque and firm."],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the chicken stock to a boil in a saucepan. Slowly whisk in the grits, stirring constantly to avoid lumps. Once combined, reduce the heat to low and cook, stirring often, for 25 to 30 minutes until the grits are creamy and tender and pull away from the sides of the pan."},{"text":"While the grits cook, fry the bacon in a large cast-iron frying pan over medium heat until crispy, about 8 minutes. Remove to a plate, leaving about 1 tbsp of bacon fat in the pan."},{"text":"Add the butter to the frying pan with the bacon fat. Once foaming, add the garlic and cook, stirring, for about 1 minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Season the shrimp well with salt and pepper. Add half to the frying pan in a single layer and cook without stirring for 2 minutes until the underside is pink. Stir and cook for a further 2 to 3 minutes until fully opaque throughout with no translucency. Transfer to a plate and repeat with the remaining shrimp."},{"text":"Once the grits are creamy, stir in the cream, lemon zest, and cooked bacon. Season to taste with salt, pepper, and lemon juice."},{"text":"Return all the shrimp to the frying pan and warm through over medium heat for about 1 minute. Top with spring onions and a drizzle of hot sauce if you like. Serve the shrimp alongside the creamy grits."}],"id":"0355182e-0165-4aa7-8187-6a28533cf282"},{"notes":["Make it ahead: prep the vegetables and pat the chicken dry the night before, then assemble and roast when you get home.","Swap in whatever root veg you have, parsnips, sweet potato or beets all work. Keep the total weight around 900 g.","Leftovers reheat brilliantly at 180°C for 15 minutes, or shred the chicken and toss with the vegetables for a next-day grain bowl.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."],"id":"f23e468e-0cd4-4fc1-b1ce-2bcb33fe91e5"},{"notes":["If fresh crawfish isn't available, frozen works just fine, thaw it first.","The roux is everything: low and slow, stirring often. Once it's dark brown, you've got the soul of the dish.","Make this ahead: the étouffée keeps for 3 days in the fridge and tastes even better reheated.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear."],"steps":[{"text":"Melt butter in a heavy-based pot over medium heat. Add flour and stir constantly, scraping the bottom, for 12 to 15 minutes until the roux is dark brown and nutty-smelling. This is the foundation, don't rush it or let it burn."},{"text":"Add the chopped onion, celery and capsicum to the roux. Stir well and cook until soft, about 8 minutes. Add the garlic and cook for another minute."},{"text":"Stir in the cayenne, paprika, oregano and thyme. Cook for 1 minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Pour in the chicken stock, stirring to combine. Bring to a simmer and cook for 5 minutes to let the flavours blend."},{"text":"Add the crawfish and stir gently. Simmer for 8 to 10 minutes until the crawfish is heated through. Season to taste with salt and pepper."},{"text":"Serve over steamed rice, garnished with spring onion and fresh parsley."}],"id":"1c3c4a2a-b4d7-41be-b7b2-e8625fc8551c"},{"notes":["Żur (sour rye starter) can be found in Polish or Eastern European grocery shops, or made at home by fermenting rye flour in water for 24 hours. It keeps in the fridge for weeks.","Make this a day ahead if you can, the flavours deepen overnight. Reheat gently on the stove, adding a splash of water if needed.","Good kielbasa makes this soup. Don't skip it or use thin sausages; you need that smoky, meaty backbone.","Cook mince and sausages until they reach 75°C in the centre, with no pink left."],"id":"c3cd451e-ca20-43ad-a93a-88a10f72319e"},{"notes":["Make it a day ahead: the flavours deepen as it sits. Reheat gently on the stove top.","Serve with rice and peas, or plain white rice to soak up the curry.","Can't find goat? Lamb shoulder works, though it cooks faster, check it at 60 minutes."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large, heavy pot over medium-high heat. Working in batches, brown the goat meat on all sides, about 3–4 minutes per batch. Remove and set aside."},{"text":"In the same pot, add the onions and cook, stirring, until soft and golden, about 5 minutes. Add the garlic and ginger, and cook for another minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Stir in the curry powder, cumin and turmeric, and cook for 2 minutes, stirring constantly, until the spices are dark and fragrant."},{"text":"Return the goat to the pot. Add the water and crushed tomatoes, scraping up any browned bits from the bottom. Stir in the salt, pepper and thyme. Bring to a gentle simmer until the liquid is bubbling lightly."},{"text":"Cover with a lid and simmer gently for 45 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the meat is beginning to soften."},{"text":"Add the potato chunks and the white parts of the spring onions. Simmer, covered, for a further 30–35 minutes until the meat is fall-apart tender and the potatoes are soft. Taste and adjust seasoning."},{"text":"Stir in the green parts of the spring onions just before serving. Serve hot in a deep bowl with the broth."}],"id":"7a2e12ed-07a6-447d-92b9-cffb8892e67f"},{"notes":["Marinade the paneer for at least 10 minutes, but up to 2 hours in the fridge makes it taste even better.","Use a good non-stick pan or oil-brushed stainless steel so the paneer doesn't stick. Don't move the pieces around too much, let them get a nice golden crust.","If you have wooden skewers, soak them in water for 30 minutes before threading."],"steps":[{"text":"Mix yoghurt, ginger-garlic paste, ground coriander, ground cumin, garam masala, cayenne, kashmiri chilli powder, lemon juice and salt in a bowl. Add paneer cubes and stir gently to coat. Set aside for at least 10 minutes (or up to 2 hours in the fridge)."},{"text":"Heat oil in a large frying pan or frying pan over medium-high heat until it shimmers."},{"text":"Thread paneer, onion and capsicum onto skewers, alternating pieces (or cook them separate in the pan if you don't have skewers). Working in batches, cook until the paneer is golden on all sides and the vegetables are softened and charred, about 8–10 minutes."},{"text":"Transfer to a serving plate, scatter with fresh coriander and serve hot with lemon wedges and roti or rice."}],"id":"28a653d3-8eb2-48da-8c11-395c8d9fda18"},{"notes":["Keeps in an airtight container in the fridge for 3 days.","Make ahead: prepare and slice the day before; store in an airtight container and eat chilled or bring to room temperature.","For extra fillings, try adding finely chopped mushroom or zucchini, just squeeze out excess moisture first."],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch the spinach in salted boiling water for 1 minute, then drain and squeeze dry. Mix with the grated carrot and chopped spring onion in a small bowl."},{"text":"Crack the eggs into a large bowl, add soy sauce, sesame oil and a pinch of salt, then whisk until well combined. Stir the vegetable mixture into the egg."},{"text":"Heat 1 tbsp neutral oil in a large non-stick frying pan over medium heat. When hot, pour in half the egg mixture and tilt the pan to spread it into a thin, even layer, about 3 mm thick. Cook for 2–3 minutes until the bottom is set but the top is still slightly wet."},{"text":"Starting from one long edge, roll the omelette tightly towards the opposite edge, using a spatula to help fold as you go. Slide onto a plate and set aside to cool slightly."},{"text":"Repeat with the remaining 1 tbsp oil and egg mixture to make a second roll."},{"text":"Once both rolls have cooled to room temperature, wrap them tightly in cling film and refrigerate for at least 30 minutes, this makes them easier to slice."},{"text":"Unwrap and slice each roll into 1.5 cm thick rounds. Serve chilled or at room temperature."}],"id":"457aecb1-2b00-49aa-93a6-7f201bb99f43"},{"notes":["Freeze the cooked mince mixture in portions for an even quicker future dinner, just thaw and warm through.","Swap flour tortillas for corn ones, or lettuce leaves if you're out of bread.","Greek yoghurt works just as well as sour cream here.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large frying pan over medium-high heat. Add the onion and cook, stirring occasionally, until softened, about 3 minutes."},{"text":"Add the beef mince and cook, breaking it up with a spoon as it browns, until cooked through (no pink remaining), about 5 minutes. Drain off any excess fat if needed."},{"text":"Stir in the taco seasoning, frozen corn and frozen capsicum until the seasoning is fragrant. Add the water and bring to a simmer until the sauce has thickened and the vegetables are tender."},{"text":"Cook, stirring occasionally, for 5 minutes or until the vegetables are heated through and the sauce has reduced slightly. Season with salt and pepper to taste."},{"text":"Warm the tortillas in a dry frying pan for 30 seconds each side, or wrap in foil and heat in a 160°C oven for 5 minutes."},{"text":"Set out the warm tortillas, spiced beef mixture and all the toppings. Let everyone build their own tacos."}],"id":"52cb54e1-a8fa-4e24-9723-c7ec93ad9603"},{"notes":["Make the pastry up to 2 days ahead and store in the fridge; the filling can also be made a day early and reheated gently before assembling.","If you can't find mixed mushrooms, use whatever is available, button mushrooms alone work just fine, though you'll lose some of that forest-floor flavour.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"id":"a9821d09-3604-4359-b3da-9fba6595a026"},{"notes":["Make it ahead: tabbouleh keeps in the fridge for up to 2 days in an airtight container. The flavours actually get better the next day.","Swap the fresh mint for dried mint if that's all you have, use about a third of the amount.","Serve with cos lettuce leaves or pita bread for scooping."],"id":"de8d653a-6137-4b5a-9e82-f5fe3c690b63"},{"notes":["Make ahead: prepare the dalo and taro leaves the day before, stored in separate airtight containers in the fridge.","Swap the white fish for any firm fish, use what's fresh. Trevally, snapper or bream all work beautifully.","Use coconut cream mixed with a little water if you can't find coconut milk, or stir in 2 tbsp grated fresh coconut at the end for deeper flavour.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large pot over medium heat. Add the onion and garlic, and cook gently until soft and fragrant, about 3 minutes."},{"text":"Add the dalo chunks and cook for 5 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the edges start to soften."},{"text":"Pour in the coconut milk and bring to a simmer until it is gently bubbling. Add the salt and stir well."},{"text":"Add the fish chunks and taro leaves, folding them gently into the coconut milk. Simmer for 15 to 20 minutes, until the fish is cooked through and the dalo is tender."},{"text":"Taste and adjust the salt and pepper. Serve hot in bowls with the creamy sauce spooned over."}],"id":"97683ac5-fe7f-4354-8989-26344dfcfb85"},{"notes":["If you can't find fresh lemon myrtle leaves, dried lemon myrtle powder works well, use about 1 tsp mixed with a little salt and pepper to season the cavity and skin.","The pan juices make a simple sauce: deglaze the hot tray with a splash of water or stock, scraping up the browned bits, and drizzle over the carved chicken.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."],"id":"236cf3c7-5fed-4d9b-855d-7389189ddaec"},{"notes":["Gudeg keeps well in the fridge for up to 3 days and tastes even better the next day as the flavours deepen. Reheat gently on the stove.","Serve with steamed rice and a simple fried chicken or hard-boiled eggs on the side, Yogyakarta style."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat a large, heavy-based pot or dutch oven over medium heat. Add the sliced shallots, garlic, and ginger, stirring constantly until they release their fragrance and soften, about 3 minutes."},{"text":"Stir in the ground turmeric and coriander, stirring for 1 minute more until the spices coat the aromatics."},{"text":"Add the jackfruit chunks, stirring well to combine with the spice paste, about 2 minutes."},{"text":"Pour in the coconut milk and water. Add the bay leaves, makrut lime leaves, and red chillies. Bring to a simmer until gently bubbling and fragrant."},{"text":"Reduce the heat to low and simmer gently, uncovered, for 75–90 minutes, stirring occasionally. The jackfruit will soften, fray at the edges, and turn golden as it absorbs the coconut and spices. The sauce will thicken and deepen in colour."},{"text":"Taste and adjust the salt. The gudeg is ready when the jackfruit is very tender and almost dissolving, and the sauce is rich and silky."}],"id":"cfb955bd-0d5d-4811-851b-d757fd07eb67"},{"notes":["The marinade works best overnight. Plan ahead or marinate for at least 4 hours.","Leftover carne asada is great in tacos, quesadillas or over rice the next day.","Food safety: Cook whole cuts of beef, lamb and pork to at least 63°C in the thickest part, then rest for 3 minutes; cook pork and any rolled or stuffed cuts until the juices run clear."],"id":"a691d514-ba4a-45d7-b39e-1bee77cd0117"},{"notes":["This freezes beautifully, make a double batch and freeze half. Thaw overnight and reheat gently.","Any root vegetables work here: turnip instead of swede, celeriac, or even a bit of cabbage. Swap parsnip for more carrot if that's what you've got.","If you can't find a ham bone, a ham hock works just as well, or use chicken stock and add a splash of ham or bacon for flavour."],"id":"897abfbf-dee4-43a9-a760-ceb02f369527"},{"notes":["Marinate overnight if you have the time, it'll be even more tender.","Cook over charcoal if you can. It makes all the difference, mate.","Pink lamb is the way. It's not overcooked until it's grey.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."],"steps":[{"text":"Combine olive oil, lemon juice, garlic, oregano, salt and pepper in a bowl. Add lamb and stir until coated. Cover and marinate in the fridge for at least 2 hours, or overnight."},{"text":"Thread lamb onto skewers, alternating with onion and capsicum pieces. If using wooden skewers, soak them in water first."},{"text":"Heat your grill or chargrill to high until very hot, or get your coals hot and glowing, covered in a light grey ash."},{"text":"Lay skewers on the grill and cook for 20–25 minutes, turning every 2–3 minutes to char evenly on all sides. The lamb is ready when it's charred outside and pink inside."},{"text":"Rest for 2 minutes, then serve with lemon wedges and extra oregano."}],"id":"78d72b9b-bccb-4c0b-8111-11230c7eb742"},{"notes":["Make the broth the night before and chill it; the fat will solidify on top and you can lift it off. This also lets the flavours develop further overnight.","If chicken bones are hard to find, ask the butcher, they'll usually give them to you cheap or free. Chicken feet add real gelatin and depth to the broth.","You can poach a whole chicken breast in the simmering broth for the last 20 minutes, then shred it to serve. Or buy a rotisserie chicken and shred that instead.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat a heavy-based pot or wok over medium-high heat. Place the unpeeled ginger and onion directly on the heat and char them, turning occasionally, until blackened all over, about 8–10 minutes. This gives the broth a subtle, sweet depth. Scrape away the worst of the char if you like, but don't wash it off."},{"text":"Blanch the chicken bones and pieces: bring a large pot of water to the boil, add the chicken, and simmer for 2 minutes. Drain and rinse the bones under cold water, then scrape away any scum. This keeps your broth clear and clean."},{"text":"In a clean pot, add 2 litres of water and bring to the boil. Add the blanched chicken bones, charred ginger and onion, star anise, cinnamon stick, garlic, coriander seeds and peppercorns. Bring back to the boil, then reduce the heat to low and simmer, uncovered, for at least 3 hours. The longer you go, the better, Mum simmers hers overnight. Skim off any foam that rises in the first 20 minutes."},{"text":"About 30 minutes before you want to eat, taste the broth and adjust with fish sauce and salt. Strain the broth through a fine sieve, discarding the solids."},{"text":"Cook the rice noodles according to packet directions, usually a few minutes in boiling water, until soft and tender but still slightly springy, then drain."},{"text":"To serve, place a nest of noodles in each bowl. Ladle the hot broth over the noodles, then top with shredded cooked chicken. Scatter with fresh herbs, spring onion and chilli. Serve with lime wedges and extra fish sauce on the side so people can adjust the seasoning."},{"text":"If you're making this ahead, simmer the broth the night before and refrigerate it. The next day, skim off any fat that has solidified on top, then gently reheat until steaming and bubbling lightly before serving."}],"id":"7a88a94c-c215-446d-b7f0-9764bec7d5cc"},{"steps":[{"text":"Make the filling: heat the 2 tbsp ghee in a pan over medium heat. Add the dates and cardamom, stirring often, until the dates soften into a paste, about 5 minutes. Season with salt and set aside to cool."},{"text":"Make the dough: in a bowl, cream the softened ghee with the sugar until pale, about 2 minutes. Add the flour, cardamom, cinnamon if using, salt and milk. Mix gently with your hands until the dough comes together, it should be soft but not sticky. If too dry, add a touch more milk."},{"text":"Roll: working with about 1 tbsp of dough at a time, gently roll each piece into a ball. Flatten between your palms into a disc about 6 cm across and ½ cm thick."},{"text":"Stuff: place a teaspoon of date filling in the centre of each disc. Fold the edges up and gently seal, then flatten again into a thick oval or disc shape."},{"text":"Mark: using a fork or the tip of a knife, press a gentle pattern into the top of each cookie, traditional kleicha have a decorative crosshatch or lattice. Do this gently so you don't break through."},{"text":"Heat the oven to 180°C, then bake: place the cookies on a lined baking tray, leaving a little space between each. Bake for 15–20 minutes, until the edges are golden brown but the centres still feel soft to the touch. They will firm up as they cool."},{"text":"Cool: let them rest on the tray for 5 minutes, then transfer to a wire rack. They'll crisp up at the edges while staying tender inside."}],"id":"1b2fdb2b-50c9-4094-80e6-020e5dfabc47"},{"notes":["Make ahead: prepare the filling and dough the day before, keep both covered in the fridge. Assemble and bake fresh when you're ready.","Freeze: uncooked empanadas freeze well for up to a month. Bake straight from frozen, adding 5–7 minutes to the cooking time.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"id":"4adb4a54-22c8-439e-9cc6-ed0e63b9a52e"},{"notes":["Use only the freshest, sashimi-grade ahi from a reliable fishmonger. Serve immediately after assembly.","Make the rice ahead and chill it completely before building the bowls.","Swap cucumber for daikon radish if you want a bit of crunch and bite.","The fish here is eaten raw or only cured in citrus. Buy sashimi-grade fish from a fishmonger, keep it cold and eat it the same day; skip it for anyone pregnant, very young, elderly or with low immunity.","Food safety: The seafood here is served raw or cured, so buy sashimi-grade, keep it cold and eat it the day it is made; for anyone who should avoid raw fish, cook it until opaque. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"steps":[{"text":"In a bowl, whisk together soy sauce, sesame oil, ginger and rice vinegar."},{"text":"Add the ahi cubes and gently toss to coat. Let sit in the fridge for 5 minutes, until the surface of the fish is lightly coated in the marinade."},{"text":"Divide the chilled rice between two bowls."},{"text":"Top each bowl with marinated ahi and its liquid, then layer the cucumber slices and spring onions."},{"text":"Sprinkle with sesame seeds and serve immediately with lime wedges on the side."}],"id":"acb94b1d-73d1-464f-8bfd-ee8e0974c2c5"},{"notes":["The key is bringing the fish to room temperature first, it cooks through more evenly. If your perch is larger (750 g), add 2–3 minutes to the steaming time.","If you can't find perch, use any firm white fish whole, about 500 g: bream, snapper, or barramundi work well.","Sashimi-grade fish is not needed here; a regular market-fresh white fish is perfect."],"steps":[{"text":"Remove the perch from the fridge 15 minutes before cooking to bring it to room temperature. This allows the heat to penetrate evenly. Pat dry with paper towel inside and out."},{"text":"Fill a wok or large pot with water to a depth of about 5 cm and bring to a rolling boil. Set a bamboo steamer rack or metal steaming stand inside, or use a plate on chopsticks to elevate the fish above the water."},{"text":"Place the perch on a heatproof plate that fits inside your steamer. Season lightly with salt inside the cavity and on the skin."},{"text":"Carefully transfer the plate to the steamer. Cover with a lid and steam over high heat for 10–12 minutes, until the eye of the fish turns white and cloudy and the flesh is opaque when you gently press it at the thickest part near the backbone."},{"text":"While the fish steams, warm the neutral oil in a small pan until just smoking, about 2 minutes on medium-high heat."},{"text":"Remove the fish from the steamer and transfer to a serving plate. Scatter the ginger matchsticks and spring onion lengths over the top."},{"text":"Pour the light soy sauce over the fish, then immediately pour the hot oil over the ginger and spring onion. The oil will sizzle and release their aromatics. Serve at once."}],"id":"7244a8f2-725e-4613-9ebc-d01a7c214eae"},{"notes":["Serve hot for a traditional bowl, or chill it and serve cold on a warm day.","The balance of tamarind and sugar is personal, start with less sugar and add to taste as you simmer."],"id":"d05a6711-289d-431f-892a-5d8286371d65"},{"notes":["This tastes better the next day, make it ahead and reheat gently.","If pearl onions are hard to find, use small shallots or just brown onion cut into chunks; the cooking time stays the same.","Serve with crusty bread, rice, or soft polenta to soak up the incredible sauce.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."],"id":"54c76c97-af1e-4c1c-a1a7-46fabf958d83"},{"notes":["Slice the steak against the grain, it'll be more tender. If it's too thick, pop it in the freezer for 15 minutes first to make it easier to slice.","Any supermarket steak works: the thinner you slice it, the less time it needs. Rib eye stays juicy, sirloin is leaner.","Make the marinade ahead and marinate the beef for up to 2 hours if you want deeper flavour, just let it come to room temperature before cooking.","Food safety: Steaks and roasting cuts of beef or lamb can be served pink: about 60°C in the centre is medium-rare, 70°C is well done."],"id":"1320fb7f-5dd0-45dc-b17d-d07af64429ea"},{"notes":["This soup freezes beautifully and tastes even better the next day. Make a double batch on Sunday and freeze in portions for busy weeknights.","If you can't find egusi, you can substitute with equal parts ground almonds and sesame seeds, though the texture won't be quite the same.","Food safety: Steaks and roasting cuts of beef or lamb can be served pink: about 60°C in the centre is medium-rare, 70°C is well done."],"id":"5805f2b1-531d-46df-b62f-5dcc1190af5d"},{"notes":["Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."],"id":"a7757b8c-49d7-4d6b-9355-d0c29902619a"},{"notes":["Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."],"id":"87d39f8d-6fe1-4c45-8709-46daa2413db7"},{"notes":["Larb is best served warm or at room temperature. If you can't find toasted rice powder at the markets, toast and grind white rice yourself, it only takes five minutes.","The heat is important: adjust the chillies to your taste, but the dish should have a good kick. Nok doesn't hold back.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."],"id":"7b31ff4e-ba6a-483d-91db-a2cc22ae5d84"}]$q$::jsonb)), up as (update public.published_recipes p set recipe = p.recipe || (d.r - 'id' - 'notes') || case when d.r ? 'notes' then jsonb_build_object('notes', coalesce((select jsonb_agg(n) from jsonb_array_elements(d.r->'notes') n where btrim(n#>>'{}') <> 'Food safety:'),'[]'::jsonb)) else '{}'::jsonb end, diets = case when d.r ? 'diets' then array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(d.r->'diets')) else p.diets end from d where p.id=(d.r->>'id')::uuid returning p.id) select (select count(*) from d) expected, (select count(*) from up) updated;